<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Carambolo treasure</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 7th–6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Carambolo Treasure</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The El Carambolo treasure is a hoard of 21 gold objects found in 1958 on the El Carambolo hill at Camas, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Dated broadly to the 7th and 6th centuries BCE and including pectorals, bracelets and a necklace with seal-shaped pendants, it is the best-known example of Tartessian goldwork. Its style blends local Iberian and Phoenician traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-carambolo-treasure.jpg" alt="El Carambolo treasure" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Carambolo_Treasury_-_7th-5th_cent._b.C._-_Seville_-_Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_de_Sevilla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
